<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicolás de Ovando</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1460–<a href="/years/1511">1511</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicolás de Ovando was a Spanish knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-alcantara">Order of Alcántara</a> and governor of the Indies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> from 1502 to 1509. He rebuilt Santo Domingo after a hurricane, organised the forced labour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> and crushed their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, notably by the massacre at Jaragua in 1503 and the war against Higüey. His rule made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> the base of Spanish power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-ovando.jpg" alt="Nicolás de Ovando" width="319" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicol%C3%A1s_de_Ovando_y_C%C3%A1ceres.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ovando was born around 1460 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>, the second son of Captain Diego de Cáceres Ovando and Isabel de Flores Gutierrez. Some place his birth at Brozas, but the chronicler Gonzalo Fernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, who knew him well, called him a native of Cáceres. He entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-alcantara">Order of Alcántara</a>, a military order founded in 1156 that had fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>, and rose to the rank of Commander-Major. As Commander of Lares he became a favourite of the Catholic Monarchs, above all Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 3 September 1501, after complaints from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> and others about Francisco de Bobadilla, Isabella named Ovando to replace him as Governor of the Indies. He sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 13 February 1502 with thirty ships, the largest fleet yet sent to the New World, carrying around 2,500 colonists chosen as a cross-section of Spanish society, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a>, with 13 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>. The fleet reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> in April 1502.</p><p>Finding the natives in revolt, Ovando and his subordinates suppressed them in bloody campaigns, including the Jaragua and Higüey massacres, and sent disorderly colonists back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in chains. According to las Casas, slaughter, enslavement and disease had cut the native population to 60 000 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1507, and the decline went on; some sources estimate that hundreds of thousands died forced to dig gold. After conquests by his lieutenants <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-esquivel">Juan de Esquivel</a> he founded several towns, developed mining, introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a> and sent out expeditions across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Keeping a deathbed promise to Isabella, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> recalled Ovando in 1509 to answer for his treatment of the native people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-columbus">Diego Columbus</a> succeeded him, but the crown let Ovando keep the property he brought back. He probably spent his last years at Brozas, the seat of his order. In February 1511 Ferdinand ordered him to join a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> that never took place. Ovando died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> on 29 May 1511 and was buried in the monastery of San Benito de Alcántara.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicolás de Ovando” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Columbus had reached the Taíno chiefdoms of the Caribbean in 1492: their caciques included Guacanagarí, who helped him when the Santa María was wrecked, and Anacaona, whom the governor <b>Nicolás de Ovando</b> had hanged in 1503.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1503 Governor <b>Nicolás de Ovando</b> invited Jaragua's chiefs to a feast, then had them burnt and Anacaona hanged.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-jaragua">Cacicazgo of Jaragua</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Nicolás de Ovando</b>, governor from 1502, the Taíno chiefdoms were destroyed and the island became the base of Spanish power in the Caribbean.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-santo-domingo">Captaincy General of Santo Domingo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Brozas, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.61,-6.78&amp;year=1493" aria-label="Brozas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4383 745 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nicolás de Ovando"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4833 1015h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4833 1015h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.61,-6.78&amp;year=1493">What was here in 1493? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
